# OOP in Java - Lecture 4: Inheritance

These notes summarize the core ideas of inheritance in Java. They are designed for classroom study and repository use, not a verbatim transcript of the lecture video.

## 1) What is inheritance?

Inheritance is one of the main pillars of OOP. It lets one class reuse the properties and behavior of another class.

- Parent class (superclass) = base class
- Child class (subclass) = derived class
- The subclass inherits fields and methods from the superclass.
- The subclass can add new features and also override behavior.

Example:

- `Animal` is a parent class.
- `Dog` and `Cat` are child classes.

This reduces duplicate code and makes the design cleaner.

## 2) `extends` keyword

A class inherits from another class using the `extends` keyword.

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating...");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking...");
    }
}
```

Here, `Dog` inherits `eat()` from `Animal` and also defines its own method `bark()`.

## 3) Why inheritance matters

Inheritance helps us:

- reuse code
- model real-world relationships
- organize code into a hierarchy
- reduce duplication
- share common behavior across many classes

## 4) Constructor chaining in inheritance

When a subclass object is created, Java calls the parent constructor first.

This is called constructor chaining.

```java
class Person {
    String name;

    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class Employee extends Person {
    int id;

    Employee(String name, int id) {
        super(name);
        this.id = id;
    }
}
```

Important points:

- `super(...)` must be the first statement inside the subclass constructor.
- It calls the parent constructor.
- If you do not write `super(...)`, Java inserts a no-argument `super()` automatically.

## 5) `super` keyword

`super` is used to refer to the parent class.

It can be used for:

- calling the parent constructor: `super(...)`
- accessing parent methods: `super.methodName()`
- accessing parent fields: `super.fieldName`

Example:

```java
class Employee extends Person {
    void printInfo() {
        System.out.println(super.name);
    }
}
```

## 6) Method overriding

A subclass can redefine a method that already exists in the parent class.

This is called method overriding.

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}
```

Rules:

- same method name
- same parameter list
- same return type (or covariant return type)
- access modifier cannot be more restrictive

Using `@Override` is recommended because it helps catch mistakes early.

## 7) Upcasting and polymorphism

A subclass object can be treated as its parent type.

```java
Animal a = new Dog();
```

This is called upcasting. It is useful in OOP because the same parent reference can point to different child objects.

Example:

```java
Animal[] animals = {new Dog(), new Cat()};

for (Animal animal : animals) {
    animal.sound();
}
```

This is polymorphism: the same method call behaves differently depending on the object type.

## 8) `protected` access modifier

`protected` allows access:

- inside the same class
- inside subclasses
- inside the same package (in many cases)

It is often used when parent class data needs to be accessed by child classes without exposing it to everyone.

## 9) Inheritance types in Java

Java supports:

- single inheritance: one class extends one parent
- multilevel inheritance: class A -> class B -> class C
- hierarchical inheritance: multiple child classes extend one parent

Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes, because it can create ambiguity.

## 10) Real-world example

A company structure often uses inheritance:

- `Person`
- `Employee` extends `Person`
- `Manager` extends `Employee`
- `Developer` extends `Employee`

This is a natural model because all employees share common data like name and age, while managers and developers add their own specialized features.

## 11) Key takeaways

- Inheritance promotes code reuse.
- A subclass inherits behavior and fields from its parent.
- `super()` calls the parent constructor.
- Overriding changes parent behavior in child classes.
- Upcasting enables polymorphism.
- Inheritance helps design scalable and organized object models.

